6.1 It will print different text because using == with Strings compares their memory addresses
(reference equality) rather than their actual text content. Since the two Strings are stored at
different locations in memory, == evaluates to false. To compare the actual content of Strings,
you should use the .equals() method.

6.2 Yes, you explained it well! An array index must always be strictly less than the array's length
because arrays are 0-indexed (meaning the first element is at index 0 and the last is at length - 1).
If an index equals or exceeds the length, it points to a memory location outside the array's allocated
space, resulting in an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException..

